# LinuxShot desktop entry. Two things in this file are identity, not cosmetics,
# and getting either wrong breaks the GNOME screen-capture permission. See
# build/linux/README.md for the mechanism and for how to test it.
#
# 1. The file name. xdg-desktop-portal derives our app id from the systemd user
#    unit we run in, and a launch by gnome-shell or gnome-session autostart puts
#    us in app-gnome-io.github.minnyat.linuxshot-<pid>.scope - so the app id
#    follows THIS FILE'S NAME. It must stay io.github.minnyat.linuxshot.desktop
#    in every channel (AppImage, .deb, .rpm, Flatpak, tarball, autostart),
#    matching singleInstanceID in main.go. A channel that ships the same content
#    as linuxshot.desktop yields app id "linuxshot" instead: a second permission
#    prompt, a second stored permission, and revoking one leaves the other.
#    Worse, if BOTH entries are ever installed, gnome-shell resolves the
#    StartupWMClass "linuxshot" to the entry whose own id matches it
#    (startup_wm_class_is_exact_match, shell-app-system.c), i.e. to
#    linuxshot.desktop - so an instance launched from the correct entry has
#    portal id io.github.minnyat.linuxshot while the shell calls the focused
#    window linuxshot, and the request is refused outright. Never ship a second
#    entry carrying StartupWMClass=linuxshot.
#
# 2. StartupWMClass=linuxshot - exact, lowercase. Our window carries
#    WM_CLASS = "linuxshot", "Linuxshot" (from g_set_prgname("linuxshot"), which
#    Wails sets from the Linux ProgramName option in main.go) and no
#    _GTK_APPLICATION_ID. The shell looks the window's WM_CLASS up in a
#    string-keyed table of StartupWMClass values, so the match is case-sensitive
#    and it is the lowercase instance part that matches. Without it the shell
#    cannot map our window to this entry, so it does not consider us the focused
#    app and xdg-desktop-portal refuses the request.
#
# StartupNotify is false because nothing here needs it: GTK completes the shell's
# startup sequence on our first window map either way (Wails maps the toplevel
# synchronously, before the hide that minimize-to-tray queues), so the key only
# controls launch feedback - which with minimize-to-tray would point at a window
# that immediately disappears.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Version=1.0
Name=LinuxShot
GenericName=Screenshot Tool
Comment=Capture your screen, annotate it, and share the result
Exec=linuxshot
Icon=io.github.minnyat.linuxshot
Terminal=false
Categories=Graphics;2DGraphics;RasterGraphics;
Keywords=screenshot;screen;capture;snip;snapshot;annotate;region;print screen;
StartupNotify=false
StartupWMClass=linuxshot
